(* ==========================================================================
   Butter VM — Instruction Set Architecture
   A stack-based virtual machine that is the compilation target for the
   Butter AST defined in GRAMMAR.bnf. Implemented in src/value.zig,
   src/chunk.zig, src/vm.zig, and src/compiler.zig.
   ========================================================================== *)


(* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   1. DESIGN GOALS ("RISC applied to a stack machine")
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- *)

(* A classic RISC ISA gets its simplicity from three commitments: every
   instruction is the same width and decodes the same way, instructions
   are load/store only (arithmetic never touches memory directly, only
   registers), and the instruction set is small and orthogonal (one job
   per instruction, compose the rest in software). This VM applies the
   same three commitments to a stack machine instead of a register machine:

   a) FIXED-WIDTH INSTRUCTIONS. Every instruction is the same
      `struct { op: OpCode, operand: u32 }` (see src/chunk.zig), regardless
      of what `op` is. There is no variable-length encoding and no
      instruction that carries more than one operand. Unused operands are
      simply zero. This trades code density (a real byte-packed encoding
      would be smaller) for a trivial, uniform decode step — appropriate
      for a teaching/target VM where correctness and simplicity dominate.
      LOAD_INDEX/STORE_INDEX (section 3) need two compile-time constants
      (an array's base slot and its length) but keep to this same one-u32
      shape by packing both into the one operand (16 bits each) rather
      than growing Instruction a second field — see `chunk.packIndexOperand`.

   b) LOAD/STORE ONLY. Arithmetic, comparison, and logical instructions
      (ADD, LT, NOT, ...) take zero operands and work purely on values
      already on the stack; the only instructions that name a location
      (a constant-pool slot or a local-variable slot) are the LOAD/STORE
      family (PUSH_CONST, LOAD_LOCAL, STORE_LOCAL). Nothing else reaches
      into the constant pool or a variable slot.

   c) ORTHOGONAL AND MINIMAL. There is exactly one conditional-branch
      instruction (JUMP_IF_FALSE) and one unconditional one (JUMP); a
      JUMP_IF_TRUE was deliberately omitted because 'or' is expressible as
      JUMP_IF_FALSE + JUMP (see section 4), the same way real RISC ISAs
      favor a minimal set of branch primitives that software sequences
      into everything else.

   Now that the source language has functions (GRAMMAR.bnf, design notes
   3b-d), the VM has a call stack: every function's LOAD_LOCAL/STORE_LOCAL
   operands are slot indices *relative to the current call frame's base
   pointer*, not absolute (see sections 5 and 6). Top-level code is simply
   frame 0, with its base pointer fixed at 0 for the program's whole
   lifetime — which is why nothing about existing top-level compilation
   changed when functions were added.

   There is also no type-checking pass: the compiler resolves variable
   *names* to slots at compile time but does not check that a variable's
   declared type agrees with what's stored into it, and the VM checks
   *runtime* value types operation-by-operation (section 3). Static type
   checking would be a separate semantic-analysis pass layered on top of
   the existing parser output; it isn't attempted here. *)


(* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   2. VALUE REPRESENTATION
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- *)

<value>    ::= INT | FLOAT | BOOL | STRING | ARRAY_REF

(* INT and FLOAT are i64 / f64. STRING values are borrowed slices into a
   Chunk's constant pool (which in turn borrows from the original source
   text) — never separately allocated, copied, or freed at run time.
   Because GRAMMAR.bnf defines no string-concatenation operator and no
   growable/heap-allocated collections, every string a running program can
   ever produce already exists as a literal fixed at compile time. That
   means the VM needs no heap and no garbage collector: every `Value` is
   either a plain scalar, a slice it never owns, or (ARRAY_REF) a
   reference to storage that lives elsewhere on this same value stack —
   see the addendum below and section 6's generic-array addendum. *)

(* ARRAY_REF (GRAMMAR.bnf design note 3j) is `{ base: u32, len: u32 }` —
   `base` an ABSOLUTE index into the VM's `stack` array (not frame-relative:
   it's computed once, at MAKE_ARRAY_REF time, from whichever frame's `bp`
   the referenced array actually lives in — section 6), `len` its length.
   It is the runtime representation of a GENERIC (unsized, `int[]`-style)
   array parameter or return value ONLY; a fixed-size array (design note
   3e) never produces one — see the addendum right below this one for why
   those two remain deliberately different runtime representations. There
   is no way to construct an ARRAY_REF from Butter source directly (no
   syntax makes one appear as a literal); the only way one comes into
   being is MAKE_ARRAY_REF, always compiler-generated at a call site
   passing a fixed-size local into a generic parameter (`compiler.zig`'s
   `compileGenericArrayArgument`). *)

(* A compiled program is one main Chunk (the top-level code, run as an
   implicit frame 0 — see section 6) plus one Chunk per declared function,
   each with its OWN constant pool. Nothing is shared between them:
   per GRAMMAR.bnf design note 3b, a function body can only ever reference
   its own parameters/locals, never a top-level variable or another
   function's locals, so there is no reason for their constant pools or
   local-slot numbering to overlap or coordinate. *)

(* Arrays (GRAMMAR.bnf design note 3e) are NOT a `<value>` variant — there
   is no runtime "array value" that gets pushed, popped, or passed around
   as a single unit. An array is simply `<declared size>` consecutive
   stack slots (section 5), and every individual slot still holds one
   plain scalar `<value>` exactly as before. This is what lets arrays fit
   into a VM with no heap and no garbage collector: the array itself isn't
   a new kind of runtime object, just a compile-time-known grouping of the
   same slots locals always lived in. *)

(* `len(arr)` (GRAMMAR.bnf design note 3i) on a FIXED-size local compiles
   straight to PUSH_CONST of the array's declared size — the compiler
   already has that number in hand (it's `Local.array`'s `.fixed` payload
   — section 5), so `len` needs no dedicated opcode there and does nothing
   at runtime beyond what any other INT literal does; the constant folding
   happens entirely at compile time. On a GENERIC local (design note 3j),
   there IS no compile-time number to fold to — the actual length depends
   on whatever array the caller passed — so `len(arr)` instead compiles to
   LOAD_REF_LEN, a genuine runtime read of the reference's own `len` field
   (section 3). *)


(* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   3. INSTRUCTION SET
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- *)

(* Stack effect notation: ( before -- after ), stack grows to the right. *)

   PUSH_CONST   #idx     ( -- v )            push constants[idx]
   PUSH_TRUE              ( -- true )
   PUSH_FALSE             ( -- false )
   POP                    ( v -- )            discard the top of stack

   LOAD_LOCAL   #slot     ( -- v )            push stack[slot]
   STORE_LOCAL  #slot     ( v -- v )          stack[slot] = v (peeks, does
                                               not consume — this is what
                                               lets 'x := 5' be used as an
                                               expression, per GRAMMAR.bnf's
                                               <assignment> production)

   LOAD_INDEX   #packed(slot,length)  ( index -- v )
                                               pops index (must be INT);
                                               TypeMismatch otherwise.
                                               IndexOutOfBounds unless
                                               0 <= index < length. Else
                                               pushes stack[slot + index]
                                               (slot/length both unpacked
                                               from the one operand — see
                                               section 1a).
   STORE_INDEX  #packed(slot,length)  ( index v -- v )
                                               pops v, then index (same
                                               Type/BoundsError checks as
                                               LOAD_INDEX), sets
                                               stack[slot + index] = v,
                                               then pushes v back — the
                                               same peek-not-consume shape
                                               as STORE_LOCAL, so
                                               'arr[i] := v' is usable as
                                               an expression too. The
                                               index must be compiled
                                               (and therefore pushed)
                                               BEFORE the value, which is
                                               why the stack effect reads
                                               index-then-v rather than
                                               v-then-index.

   MAKE_ARRAY_REF #packed(slot,length)  ( -- ref )
                                               pushes a fresh
                                               ARRAY_REF{ base = bp + slot,
                                               len = length } (slot/length
                                               both compile-time constants,
                                               unpacked the same way
                                               LOAD_INDEX/STORE_INDEX's
                                               operand is). Synthesizes a
                                               reference to a FIXED-size
                                               local at `slot` — GRAMMAR.bnf
                                               design note 3j — only ever
                                               emitted when passing such a
                                               local into a generic (`[]`)
                                               parameter.
   LOAD_INDEX_REF #slot   ( index -- v )      pops index (must be INT);
                                               reads ref = stack[bp+slot]
                                               (must be ARRAY_REF —
                                               TypeMismatch otherwise);
                                               IndexOutOfBounds unless
                                               0 <= index < ref.len; else
                                               pushes stack[ref.base+index].
                                               The generic-array counterpart
                                               to LOAD_INDEX: `slot` names
                                               where the REFERENCE lives,
                                               not the array itself — its
                                               base/length come from the
                                               reference at runtime instead
                                               of the operand.
   STORE_INDEX_REF #slot  ( index v -- v )    generic-array counterpart to
                                               STORE_INDEX, same reference
                                               indirection and peek-not-
                                               consume shape as
                                               LOAD_INDEX_REF/STORE_INDEX.
   LOAD_REF_LEN  #slot    ( -- int )          reads ref = stack[bp+slot]
                                               (must be ARRAY_REF); pushes
                                               ref.len as an INT. What
                                               `len(arr)` compiles to for a
                                               GENERIC array, since (unlike
                                               a fixed-size one) its length
                                               isn't a compile-time constant
                                               to fold to.

   ADD, SUB, MUL, DIV, MOD, POW
                          ( a b -- r )        both operands must be numeric
                                               (INT or FLOAT); INT op INT
                                               stays INT (overflow-checked);
                                               any FLOAT operand promotes
                                               both to FLOAT. DIV/MOD by
                                               zero and INT overflow are
                                               runtime errors, not traps.

   NEG                    ( a -- r )          numeric only
   NOT                    ( a -- r )          BOOL only

   EQ, NEQ                ( a b -- bool )     defined for every pair of
                                               value types: numeric operands
                                               compare across INT/FLOAT by
                                               value; any other mismatched
                                               pair of types simply compares
                                               unequal (this mirrors '=='
                                               in most dynamically-checked
                                               languages, rather than being
                                               a type error)
   LT, LTE, GT, GTE        ( a b -- bool )    numeric operands only

   JUMP         #target               ip := target
   JUMP_IF_FALSE #target  ( bool -- bool )    peeks (does not pop) the
                                               condition; if false, ip :=
                                               target. Every use site is
                                               responsible for POPping the
                                               condition itself on both the
                                               taken and fall-through paths
                                               (section 4) — this is the one
                                               instruction in the ISA with a
                                               "conditional" stack effect,
                                               and confining that to a single
                                               well-tested opcode is the
                                               payoff for keeping everything
                                               else unconditional.
                                               TypeMismatch if the peeked
                                               value is not BOOL — this is
                                               the sole place 'if'/'while'
                                               conditions and 'and'/'or'
                                               operands are checked to
                                               actually be booleans.

   CALL         #func_index  ( arg1 ... argN -- result )
                                               the N arguments (N = the
                                               callee's arity) are already
                                               on the stack, pushed by
                                               ordinary expression codegen
                                               before CALL executes; CALL
                                               itself pushes nothing. It
                                               suspends the current chunk
                                               (saving its ip and base
                                               pointer as a new frame — see
                                               section 6) and jumps to
                                               functions[func_index]'s own
                                               chunk at instruction 0, with
                                               the base pointer set so the
                                               callee's LOAD_LOCAL/
                                               STORE_LOCAL slot 0 is exactly
                                               where arg1 already sits.
                                               CallStackOverflow if the
                                               frame stack is already at
                                               capacity.
   RET                     ( v -- v )          pops the current frame
                                               (discarding every argument
                                               and local it owns — see
                                               section 6), then leaves v,
                                               the value the returning
                                               function computed, as the
                                               sole result on the caller's
                                               stack. Every function chunk
                                               is guaranteed by the
                                               compiler to end in a RET
                                               (see GRAMMAR.bnf design note
                                               3c) — this is the only way a
                                               function chunk's execution
                                               ever ends.

   PRINT                  ( v -- )            pop v, write it followed by
                                               a newline

   HALT                                       stop execution (only ever
                                               appears in the main chunk,
                                               at the very end — see
                                               section 6)


(* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   4. CODEGEN PATTERNS (how the compiler lowers control flow and short
      circuiting onto the instructions above — the standard "backpatched
      jump" technique)
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- *)

   if-stmt (condition, then, optional else):

       <condition>
       JUMP_IF_FALSE  else_target
       POP                          (* discard a truthy condition *)
       <then-branch>
       JUMP  end_target
     else_target:
       POP                          (* discard a falsy condition — this pop
                                        happens whether or not there is an
                                        else-branch, which is what keeps the
                                        stack balanced either way *)
       [ <else-branch> ]
     end_target:

   while-stmt:

     loop_start:
       <condition>
       JUMP_IF_FALSE  exit_target
       POP
       <body>
       JUMP  loop_start
     exit_target:
       POP

   'and' (short-circuits to a false left operand without evaluating right):

       <left>
       JUMP_IF_FALSE  end_target    (* left false -> skip right, its false
                                        value is the result *)
       POP                          (* left true -> discard it *)
       <right>
     end_target:

   'or' (short-circuits to a true left operand without evaluating right;
   built from JUMP + JUMP_IF_FALSE rather than a dedicated JUMP_IF_TRUE,
   per the minimality goal in section 1c):

       <left>
       JUMP_IF_FALSE  else_target
       JUMP  end_target             (* left true -> skip right, its true
                                        value is the result *)
     else_target:
       POP                          (* left false -> discard it *)
       <right>
     end_target:


(* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   5. VARIABLE RESOLUTION (compile time, no runtime name lookup)
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- *)

   The compiler keeps a list of (name, scope_depth, slot, width) entries
   in declaration order, separately per chunk being compiled (the main
   chunk, and then each function's chunk in turn — see section 6). `slot`
   is tracked explicitly (a running `next_slot` counter) rather than
   implied by position in that list, because `width` isn't always 1: a
   plain scalar local occupies exactly one slot, but an array local
   (GRAMMAR.bnf design note 3e) occupies `<declared size>` consecutive
   slots starting at `slot`, so slot numbers and list position diverge as
   soon as any array is in scope. Entering a <block> increases
   scope_depth; leaving it removes every local declared at that depth
   from the compiler's list, decrements `next_slot` by each removed
   local's width (so a later sibling scope reuses the same slots instead
   of growing the frame unboundedly), and emits one POP per slot removed
   (an array-local's whole width, not just one POP) so the runtime stack
   shrinks back in step with the compile-time model. A reference to an
   undeclared name, or an assignment to one, is a compile error
   (`CompileError.UndefinedVariable`) — one of the few forms of semantic
   checking this compiler does perform, since it is required just to
   assign slots at all, not an optional extra. Shadowing an outer variable
   in an inner block is allowed (lookup walks the locals list from the
   end, so the innermost matching declaration wins); redeclaring the same
   name twice *within one scope* is not rejected — it simply shadows the
   earlier slot from that point on. This mirrors the Lox-family "locals
   resolved to stack slots" design (as in Crafting Interpreters' clox).

   An array-typed local's bare name resolves for LOAD_LOCAL/STORE_LOCAL
   purposes exactly like a scalar's (same lookup, same list) but using it
   that way is a compile error (`CompileError.ArrayUsedAsScalar`) — a
   FIXED-size array is only ever accessed through LOAD_INDEX/STORE_INDEX
   (section 3), which pack that same `slot` together with `width` (there
   called `length`, for the bounds check) into one instruction operand.

   `Local.array` (compiler.zig) distinguishes which of two array kinds a
   local is, or `null` for a plain scalar: `.fixed(n)` (width `n`,
   accessed via LOAD_INDEX/STORE_INDEX as above — GRAMMAR.bnf design note
   3e) or `.generic` (width exactly 1 — it's a single ARRAY_REF value, not
   raw slots — accessed via LOAD_INDEX_REF/STORE_INDEX_REF/LOAD_REF_LEN
   instead, design note 3j). The bare-name-as-scalar restriction above
   applies to BOTH kinds identically; a generic local's reference value
   still can't be loaded/stored as a plain expression outside the narrow
   contexts section 6's generic-array addendum describes (forwarding it as
   a call argument or a return).

   A function's parameters are simply its first locals, occupying slots
   0..arity-1, added to that function's locals list before its body is
   compiled at all — from the compiler's perspective a parameter is just a
   local that already has a value by the time the body starts, the same
   way a var-declaration's initializer already has a value on the stack
   before the declaration finishes (section 2). A scalar parameter costs
   one slot; a `.fixed(n)` array-typed parameter costs `n` consecutive
   slots, exactly like a fixed-size array-typed local anywhere else (see
   the array-typed-local paragraph just above); a `.generic` array-typed
   parameter costs exactly one slot too (it's a single ARRAY_REF value,
   design note 3j) — which is precisely why `arity` (the total slot width
   CALL reserves, section 6) and the parameter *count* (what the
   arity-mismatch check in GRAMMAR.bnf design note 3d actually compares)
   are two different numbers once any parameter is fixed-size-array-typed. Locals lists are entirely separate per chunk: a
   function's locals list starts fresh (containing only its own
   parameters) and is discarded once that function's chunk is done
   compiling, which is what makes it structurally impossible for a
   function body to resolve a top-level variable's name — the compiler
   never even has that name in scope while compiling the function (per
   GRAMMAR.bnf design note 3b).


(* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   6. FUNCTION CALLS & RECURSION
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- *)

   Each declared function compiles to its own `chunk_mod.Function` — a
   name, an arity, a return_width, and a self-contained Chunk (own
   instructions, own constant pool). A compiled `Program` is one main
   Chunk (top-level code) plus a slice of these, indexed by the operand
   CALL instructions use to name their target (resolved by name to that
   index at compile time — see compiler.zig's function table, built before
   any function body is compiled so that calls resolve regardless of
   declaration order, including a function calling itself or two functions
   calling each other).

   ARITY AND RETURN_WIDTH ARE STACK-SLOT COUNTS, NOT VALUE COUNTS. `arity`
   is the total number of stack slots every argument together occupies —
   a scalar argument is 1 slot, an array argument is `<declared size>`
   slots (GRAMMAR.bnf design note 3e) — and `return_width` is likewise how
   many slots the return value occupies (1 for a scalar return, the
   declared size for an array return). Neither is the same as counting
   *values*: a call with one array-typed and one scalar parameter has an
   arity of `<array size> + 1`, not 2.

   CALLING CONVENTION. A call expression's arguments compile left to
   right onto the stack, in whatever shape each one needs: a scalar
   argument is one ordinary expression (one value pushed), while an
   array-typed argument (matched against the parameter it fills) is a
   sequence of `<declared size>` LOAD_LOCALs copying that argument array's
   slots on top of the stack, one by one, in order (GRAMMAR.bnf design
   note 3e) — passing is always by VALUE, a fresh copy, never a reference
   to the caller's own slots. Either way, by the time CALL executes, the
   callee's entire argument list — `arity` slots' worth, whatever mix of
   scalars and array copies produced them — is simply sitting on top of
   the stack in order. CALL then:

     1. Checks the frame stack isn't already at capacity
        (CallStackOverflow if it is — this is what turns unbounded
        recursion into a catchable runtime error instead of a native
        stack overflow/crash).
     2. Pushes a frame recording the CALLER's chunk, ip (already advanced
        past the CALL instruction, so execution resumes right after it),
        base pointer, and return_width — everything needed to resume the
        caller once the callee returns, including how many slots the
        CALLER's own eventual RET will need to copy (so a nested call's
        RET can't clobber that number for the outer call still waiting on
        the stack, e.g. `sum(doubleAll(nums))`).
     3. Sets the new base pointer to `sp - arity`: since the callee's
        arguments already occupy the top `arity` slots of the stack, this
        makes them exactly the callee's LOAD_LOCAL/STORE_LOCAL slots
        0..arity-1 without copying them anywhere else — an array-typed
        parameter's slots are simply a contiguous sub-range of that same
        span, addressed via LOAD_INDEX/STORE_INDEX like any other
        array-typed local (section 5).
     4. Switches to the callee's chunk, jumps to instruction 0, and sets
        the active return_width to the callee's own (used by the RET that
        eventually returns from THIS call, not the caller's).

   RET generalizes "pop the result, restore the frame, push the result
   back" from exactly one value to `return_width` of them: a `return`
   statement's expression already leaves exactly `return_width` values
   sitting on top of the callee's stack region by the time RET executes
   (one PUSH_CONST/expression for a scalar return, `<declared size>`
   LOAD_LOCALs for an array return, mirroring how array arguments are
   copied at the call site). RET then copies those top `return_width`
   slots down onto the frame's own base pointer IN PLACE (slot-by-slot,
   `stack[bp+i] = stack[bp_relative_top+i]`) rather than through a pop/push
   pair, sets the stack pointer to `bp + return_width` (discarding every
   argument, parameter-slot, and local the callee ever pushed in one step —
   whether or not the compiler's own block-scope POP bookkeeping ran,
   since a `return` can fire from arbitrarily deep inside nested
   if/while/block bodies and always unwinds the whole frame directly),
   pops the current frame, and restores the chunk/ip/base pointer/
   return_width from it. For a scalar return (`return_width = 1`) this is
   exactly the old pop-then-push behavior, just written as a single-slot
   copy instead.

   RECURSION FALLS OUT FOR FREE. Nothing about CALL or RET is aware of
   whether the callee is the same function as the caller. Each call gets
   its own frame and its own base pointer into a fresh region of the
   *same* stack array, so a function calling itself (or two functions
   calling each other) just works: fib(n-1) and fib(n-2) each get their
   own base pointer, so their respective slot-0 values (their own `n`)
   never collide, no matter how deep the recursion goes — until either the
   frame stack (CallStackOverflow) or the value stack itself
   (StackOverflow) runs out of room.

   TOP LEVEL IS FRAME 0. There's no special-casing anywhere for "not
   inside a function": top-level code is simply the main chunk, executing
   with base pointer 0 for the program's entire lifetime, and it never
   needs a frame pushed for itself because nothing ever CALLs into it —
   it's where `run` starts. This is also why `return` is restricted to
   inside a function body (GRAMMAR.bnf design note 3c) rather than a
   supported way to end the program early: RET always pops a frame that a
   CALL pushed, and no CALL ever runs before the main chunk starts, so
   there is no frame to pop at the top level.

   GENERIC ARRAYS (GRAMMAR.bnf design note 3j) DON'T CHANGE ANY OF THE
   ABOVE — they just change what a "value" is at one particular slot. A
   generic (`int[]`) parameter costs exactly 1 slot for arity purposes,
   same as a scalar, because at runtime it holds exactly one `Value`: an
   ARRAY_REF (section 2). Where that reference comes FROM depends on what
   the caller passed:
     - a FIXED-size local (an array or a fixed-size parameter): the
       compiler emits MAKE_ARRAY_REF at the call site, computing
       `base = bp + <that local's slot>` using the CALLER's own bp (still
       in scope — this runs before CALL switches frames) and pushing the
       resulting reference as this one argument slot.
     - a GENERIC local (already holding a reference): LOAD_LOCAL simply
       copies the existing `ArrayRef` value onward, unchanged — its `base`
       still points wherever it always did, since a reference's target
       never moves.
   Either way, by the time CALL executes, that argument is just one more
   ordinary slot among the callee's `arity` — CALL/RET (above) don't know
   or care that a slot holds a reference rather than an INT.

   WHY A REFERENCE IS SAFE AS AN ARGUMENT BUT NOT ALWAYS SAFE AS A RETURN.
   An argument's reference is only ever *read* during the callee's own
   call, which is always fully nested inside (and therefore shorter-lived
   than) whichever frame the reference points into — so it can never go
   stale before the callee is done with it. A RETURN is different: the
   reference has to remain valid AFTER this function's own frame is
   discarded. A reference synthesized by THIS call's own MAKE_ARRAY_REF
   points into THIS frame, which RET is about to pop — returning one would
   hand the caller a reference to slots that are about to be reused by
   whatever gets called next. Only a reference this function received
   as-is (a generic parameter, or a nested call's own generic return,
   forwarded transitively) is safe to return, because by induction it was
   always ultimately synthesized as some ANCESTOR frame's call argument —
   and an ancestor frame, by definition, outlives every frame nested
   inside it. The compiler enforces this statically
   (`compiler.zig`'s `compileGenericArrayReturn`,
   `CompileError.EscapingArrayReference`) rather than leaving it as a
   runtime footgun — no ARRAY_REF a running program ever holds can be
   dangling, by construction, the same "checked at compile time, not
   trusted at runtime" spirit as bounds-checking (section 3's
   LOAD_INDEX/LOAD_INDEX_REF).


(* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   7. THE FOR-LOOP (desugars to section 4's while-stmt pattern)
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- *)

   `for v in start..end body` (GRAMMAR.bnf design note 3g) introduces its
   own wrapper scope holding two hidden locals the compiler generates —
   the range's end bound (evaluated once) and the loop variable itself —
   then emits *exactly* the while-stmt pattern from section 4, just with
   the condition/increment hardcoded to those two locals instead of
   arbitrary compiled expressions:

       <end-expr>                    (* hidden local: end_slot *)
       <start-expr>                  (* hidden local: var_slot, = v *)
     loop_start:
       LOAD_LOCAL  var_slot
       LOAD_LOCAL  end_slot
       LT
       JUMP_IF_FALSE  exit_target
       POP
       <body>                        (* v resolves as an ordinary local *)
       LOAD_LOCAL  var_slot
       PUSH_CONST  #1
       ADD
       STORE_LOCAL  var_slot
       POP
       JUMP  loop_start
     exit_target:
       POP
       (* both hidden locals popped as the wrapper scope closes,
          section 5 *)

   No new opcodes are involved — the same way 'and'/'or' (section 4) are
   sugar over JUMP_IF_FALSE/JUMP rather than dedicated instructions, `for`
   is sugar over the exact same LOAD_LOCAL/ADD/STORE_LOCAL/JUMP primitives
   every other loop already uses. Because `end_slot`'s value is computed
   once up front and never touched again, re-assigning whatever expression
   produced `end` inside the loop body has no effect on how many
   iterations run (see the "bounds evaluated once" behavior this
   guarantees). The hidden end-bound local is given the empty string as
   its name — never a legal IDENTIFIER lexeme (GRAMMAR.bnf section 1), so
   it can never collide with, shadow, or be resolved by any name a user
   actually writes.


(* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   8. IMPORT/EXPORT (compile-time only - no new opcodes, no runtime
      concept of a "module")
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- *)

   import/export (GRAMMAR.bnf design note 3h) add precisely one new
   compile-time concern - which names a given file may call - and touch
   nothing at runtime: the VM has no notion of files or modules, CALL's
   operand is still a single flat index into one program-wide function
   table, exactly as it was before a program could span more than one
   file (section 6). Everything described here lives in src/module.zig and
   the multi-module entry point compiler.Compiler.compileModules
   (compileProgram is now a thin wrapper that hands compileModules a
   single module with an empty import list, so a plain one-file program's
   behavior - every function visible to every other, export irrelevant -
   is unchanged).

   THE MODULE LOADER RUNS BEFORE COMPILATION, NOT DURING IT. Given an
   entry program, module.Loader recursively lexes and parses every file
   it transitively imports, depth-first, BEFORE any compilation begins.
   Each file is keyed by a lexically normalized path (resolved relative to
   the *importing* file's own directory, GRAMMAR.bnf design note 3h) and
   memoized in a hash map: the second time any import path resolves to a
   key already in that map, the loader returns the already-parsed Module
   immediately instead of re-reading/re-lexing/re-parsing the file. This
   memoization is the entire mechanism behind "a shared dependency is
   compiled exactly once" - by the time compileModules ever sees the
   module list, a diamond-shaped import graph has already been collapsed
   down to one entry per distinct file, appearing exactly once in
   Loader.order. compileModules does not itself deduplicate anything; it
   simply never gets asked to compile the same module twice.

   A cycle (file A importing file B importing file A, however indirectly)
   is caught during this same loading pass - the loader keeps a stack of
   paths currently mid-load, and a path re-encountered while still on that
   stack (not yet fully loaded, so not yet in the memoization map either)
   is LoadError.CircularImport rather than infinite recursion.

   FUNCTION REGISTRATION IS GLOBAL, VISIBILITY IS PER-MODULE.
   compileModules's first pass walks every module's function declarations
   (in whatever order `modules` lists them - order doesn't matter here
   since this pass only builds a name table, the same as single-file
   compileProgram always has) and registers each one with its owning
   module's index and `exported` flag alongside the name/arity/call-index
   every function has always had. A name collision anywhere in this whole
   table - even between two files that don't import each other at all -
   is SemanticError.DuplicateFunction, the same error a single file's own
   two `func f()`s already were (GRAMMAR.bnf design note 3h: this is what
   keeps CALL's resolution a flat table rather than needing per-module
   namespacing).

   Compiling each module's function *bodies* (and the entry module's
   top-level statements) tracks which module is "current" and which other
   modules' indices are directly reachable from it (that module's own
   `imports` list). A call to name `f` resolves in two steps: first find
   `f` in the global table (as always - SemanticError.UndefinedFunction if
   no such name exists anywhere), then check it's *visible* from here -
   either it belongs to the current module (always allowed, exported or
   not: a module's own private helpers are always usable by itself), or
   it belongs to a module in the current module's direct-import list AND
   is exported (SemanticError.FunctionNotVisible otherwise). This is
   deliberately NOT transitive: importing B does not make B's own
   imports' exports visible here (GRAMMAR.bnf design note 3h) - there is
   no automatic re-export.
   ========================================================================== *)
